msgid "`native` off sets `WEBLATE_NO_EMAIL_AUTH=1`, which removes the e-mail and password form, so with SSO on the sign-in offers Keycloak only."
msgstr ""

msgid "`native`: `ENABLE_LOGIN_FORM`. With it off, the sign-in page shows only the Keycloak entry; with `ldap` on it shows the LDAP form without the e-mail toggle. `ENABLE_PASSWORD_AUTH` keeps its default `true`, because the LDAP form and the break-glass path sign in through the password endpoints, so the e-mail sign-in stays reachable through `/auth?form=1` and `POST /api/v1/auths/signin`: the form is shared."
msgstr ""

msgid "`native`: the application setting `password_authentication_enabled_for_web`, converged at every deploy by [files/ruby/apply_login_methods.rb](./files/ruby/apply_login_methods.rb). While it is off, the sign-in page shows only the Keycloak entry and GitLab refuses password sign-ins on the web."
msgstr ""

msgid "`native`: the system parameter `auth_signup.reset_password`; Odoo has no setting that hides its password form, so [`templates/javascript.js.j2`](./templates/javascript.js.j2) hides the login and password fields, the \"Log in\" button and the \"Reset Password\" link while `native` and `ldap` are both off. Local and LDAP accounts share one form and Odoo tries the local password before the directory, so with `ldap` on and `native` off the form still admits local accounts."
msgstr ""

msgid "`native`: while `sso` is on and both `native` and `ldap` are off, Element's `sso_redirect_options` (`on_welcome_page`, `on_login_page`) hand its welcome and sign-in pages straight to Keycloak, so Element never renders the password form. Synapse keeps password login on its client API, because the platform administrator, the bot accounts and the role-group reconcile sign in with local passwords. The form is shared: local and LDAP accounts sign in through the same Element form, and native off cannot refuse local accounts while LDAP stays on."
